1 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504596 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504437 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504596 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186504460 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504596 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504596 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504596 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186504311 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504596 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f3299ff3-e06a-576d-9e6b-49153eef928c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504609 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504546 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504609 #edb/ref 1069 "q-346221" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504609 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504609 #edb/ref 1071 "What is ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\cdot \\,\\frac{\\pi }{6n}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504609 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "174ba687-7b9b-5ccb-9e0f-e16e772aa04c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504609 #edb/ref 1074 "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),$ the lower bound is $a=0$ and the upper bound is $b=\\pi /6.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{\\pi /6-0}{n}=\\frac{\\pi }{6n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f(0+i\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f(\\frac{\\pi i}{6n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi i}{6n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi i}{2n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504619 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504489 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504619 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504504 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504619 #edb/ref 1069 "q-346198" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504619 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504619 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{log}_{2}\\,(5(3+\\frac{4k}{n}))\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504619 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "368aa1bb-68dd-5e11-83de-3c6725fca7d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504619 #edb/ref 1074 "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=3.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{4}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{n}=\\frac{b-3}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=7.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=3+\\frac{4k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})={log}_{2}\\,(5{x}_{k}).$ By replacing the sample point ${x}_{k}$ with the variable $x,$ we find the original function.\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{3}^{7}{log}_{2}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[{log}_{2}\\,(5(3+\\frac{4k}{n}))\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)={log}_{2}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{and}\\,b=7.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504624 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504488 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504624 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504554 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504624 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504592 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504624 #edb/ref 1069 "q-346154" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504624 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504624 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/18/185772b358771a524b784b06aee22a623d709b15b55d9e34000e2ef106b1e7d8.png)\n\nFor the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}+1$ shown above, what is the expression for the area $A$ as a definite integral?\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $A$ | $=$ | $\\int $ | {{field-2}} | $\\text{d}x$ |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} |  |  |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504624 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4060660e-a98e-5a45-8a1f-74d923cbf4d9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504624 #edb/ref 1074 "We want to express the area $A$ between the function $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}+1$ and the $x$-axis, between $x=1$ and $x=2.$\n\nThis can be expressed as the definite integral of $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}+1$ between the lower limit $x=1$ and the upper limit $x=2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{x}+1\\,\\text{d}x\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504632 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504632 #edb/ref 1069 "q-113883" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504632 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504632 #edb/ref 1071 "What is ${\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a left Riemann sum?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504632 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5b26923d-aa9d-5c1e-919b-9d716067cb9d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504632 #edb/ref 1074 "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)={x}^{4},$ the lower bound is $a=1,$ and the upper bound is $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-1}{n}=\\frac{1}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+i\\cdot \\frac{1}{n})\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+\\frac{i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[{(1+\\frac{i}{n})}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504654 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504479 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504654 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504654 #edb/ref 1069 "q-346192" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504654 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504654 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[7{(1+\\frac{2k}{n})}^{-3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504654 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "92db8444-ee84-5a2b-a100-391911e28dfc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504654 #edb/ref 1074 "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=1.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{2}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{n}=\\frac{b-1}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=3.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=1+\\frac{2k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})=7{x}_{k}^{-3}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{3}7{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[7{(1+\\frac{2k}{n})}^{-3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)=7{x}^{-3}\\,\\text{and}\\,b=3.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504658 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504512 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504658 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504593 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504658 #edb/ref 1069 "q-346200" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504658 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504658 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{(1+\\frac{4k}{n})+1}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504658 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9831af65-2b7f-5270-b7a1-fe24be0274fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504658 #edb/ref 1074 "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=1.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{4}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{n}=\\frac{b-1}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=5.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=1+\\frac{4k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})=\\sqrt{{x}_{k}+1}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{5}\\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[\\sqrt{(1+\\frac{4k}{n})+1}\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\sqrt{x+1}\\,\\text{and}\\,b=5.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504672 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504594 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504672 #edb/ref 1069 "q-346173" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504672 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504672 #edb/ref 1071 "Express ${\\int }_{1}^{5}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+2)\\,\\text{d}x$ as the limit of a left Riemann sum.\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{5}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+2)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡($\n{{field-1}}\n$)+2)\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504672 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bacd6074-8287-5758-9454-33c120a66361" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504672 #edb/ref 1074 "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a left Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{5}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+2)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+2,$ the lower bound is $a=1,$ and the upper bound is $b=5.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{5-1}{n}=\\frac{4}{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{5}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x)+2)\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+i\\cdot \\frac{4}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[f(1+\\frac{4i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n-1}[(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\,1+\\frac{4i}{n}\\,)+2)\\,\\cdot \\frac{4}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504685 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504566 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504685 #edb/ref 1069 "q-113932" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504685 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504685 #edb/ref 1071 "What is ${\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504685 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d07197e2-a19c-5121-ad34-53964d711400" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504685 #edb/ref 1074 "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)={x}^{5},$ the lower bound is $a=1$ and the upper bound is $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-1}{n}=\\frac{1}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{x}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(1+k\\cdot \\frac{1}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(1+\\frac{k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[{(1+\\frac{k}{n})}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504688 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504688 #edb/ref 1069 "q-113931" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504688 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504688 #edb/ref 1071 "What is ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504688 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d31b1ab6-50b9-57eb-ba32-5357e77d62ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504688 #edb/ref 1074 "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ the lower bound is $a=0$ and the upper bound is $b=\\pi .$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{\\pi -0}{n}=\\frac{\\pi }{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(0+k\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[f(\\frac{\\pi k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi k}{n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504695 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504695 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504562 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504695 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504575 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504695 #edb/ref 1069 "q-346165" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504695 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504695 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/29/29729a2b35aa370f46f15d221764b3cf8fd6344ff475016b92a974cfb31b08c2.png)\n\nFor the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+1$ shown above, what is the expression for the area $A$ as a definite integral?\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $A$ | $=$ | $\\int $ | {{field-2}} | $\\text{d}x$ |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} |  |  |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504695 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d990e769-49e8-553e-999b-5b9c5313abd0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504695 #edb/ref 1074 "We want to express the area $A$ between the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+1$ and the $x$-axis, between $x=0$ and $x=3.$\n\nThis can be expressed as the definite integral of $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+1$ between the lower limit $x=0$ and the upper limit $x=3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{0}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+1\\,\\text{d}x\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504700 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186504583 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504700 #edb/ref 1069 "q-346208" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504700 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504700 #edb/ref 1071 "What is ${\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+2)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x$ expressed as the limit of a right Riemann sum?\n\n*Hint: Write down the missing expression in terms of $i$ and $n.$*\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+2)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[($\n{{field-1}}\n$)^{\\,3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}]$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504700 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e24c9c25-519c-53c3-84e2-e60c0348d7f8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186504700 #edb/ref 1074 "The definite integral can be defined in terms of the limit of a right Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ and $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nIn the definite integral\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+2)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nthe function is $f(x)=(x+2)^{3},$ the lower bound is $a=0$ and the upper bound is $b=2.$ So, we have\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-0}{n}=\\frac{2}{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+2)^{3}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(0+i\\cdot \\frac{2}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[f(\\frac{2i}{n})\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}] \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}[{(\\,\\frac{2i}{n}+2\\,)}^{\\,3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{n}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
